package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story about the archive of unfinished letters. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch44 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_archive_of_unfinished_letters",
            title = "The Archive of Unfinished Letters",
            tagline = "A student archive team discovers why a box of undelivered letters was kept safe for decades.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Box Above the Records", listOf(
                    "When Dara volunteered to help organise the town library, she expected dusty newspapers and old maps. Instead, she found a wooden box on the highest shelf, labelled RETURNED—NOT LOST. The librarian, Mr Eze, explained that it had been moved from the old post office before the building was renovated. Nobody had opened it because the letters were private and the original instructions were unclear.",
                    "Dara suggested asking the town archivist what could legally be done. The archivist, Mrs Cole, agreed to supervise an inventory without opening sealed envelopes. Inside the box were thirty-seven letters, each marked with a date and a delivery note. A register listed the senders, but the recipient column was blank. One envelope carried a familiar street name: the lane where Dara's school now stood."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Register", listOf(
                    "Mrs Cole explained that the post office had closed after a flood damaged its sorting room. Some letters had been returned to senders, while others were held because addresses had changed. The register showed that the box was never intended to be a mystery; it was a record awaiting a proper handover. Dara and her friend Femi offered to help compare the old street names with current public maps.",
                    "They worked only with the register and envelopes' exterior details. A map from the library collection showed that several streets had been renamed when the district was rebuilt. The school lane had once been called Railway View. Beside that entry, the register contained a note: 'Community reading room—care of the caretaker.' The students wondered whether the letters belonged to a project rather than individual households."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Room That Wasn't There", listOf(
                    "The school caretaker remembered a small reading room that had operated in a converted station office before the school was built. He said local residents used it to exchange books and notices. The room had closed when the railway moved its services, but its volunteers continued meeting for a while. Dara asked Mrs Cole whether the library had any public records about the group.",
                    "A folder contained meeting minutes and a list of volunteer roles. The letters had been addressed to the reading-room coordinator, who changed every year. That explained the missing recipient names. The team found a note from the final coordinator asking the post office to hold future correspondence until a new address was confirmed. The flood interrupted the transfer, and the box remained in storage."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The New Address", listOf(
                    "The minutes named the reading group's successor: a neighbourhood learning circle that still met at the community centre. Dara and Femi visited with Mrs Cole and explained the archive discovery. The centre manager, Mr Bello, recognised the old group name and said his organisation had inherited its book collection, but not its correspondence.",
                    "He checked the centre's public history folder and found a handover note listing the first coordinator's family as custodians of the group's records. The family had donated photographs but did not know about the letters. Mr Bello offered to contact them through the centre's official address. Nobody would open or deliver a letter until the proper owners and privacy rules were established."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Family's Reply", listOf(
                    "A week later, the family replied. They were surprised but pleased that the box had survived. The coordinator, Mrs Akin, had died many years earlier, and her children remembered her speaking about letters that never reached the reading room. They asked to meet the archivist and review the collection under supervision.",
                    "Mrs Cole arranged a careful appointment. The family identified the handwriting on several envelopes as belonging to volunteers, but they did not claim ownership of every letter. The archivist explained that the letters would remain sealed unless the appropriate recipient or lawful custodian gave permission. The family agreed. They mainly wanted the box's history preserved and the volunteers acknowledged."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Map of Good Work", listOf(
                    "The family brought a photograph of the old reading room. On its back was a list of partner organisations: a youth club, a literacy group, and a small adult-learning class. Dara noticed that all three still operated in some form. The letters might contain personal information, but the outside register could help the archive tell the story of how the groups worked together.",
                    "The archivist proposed a public exhibit using the box, the register, and the photograph, without displaying private correspondence. The family approved the plan. Femi suggested adding a blank board where current residents could write what a shared reading space meant to them. The library agreed, provided contributions were moderated and people could choose not to have their names shown."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Missing Volunteer", listOf(
                    "While preparing captions, Dara found a name repeated in the minutes: Samuel Nwosu, the last volunteer who had maintained the reading room's address list. A public memorial booklet said he later moved to another town. The family remembered that he had tried to collect the held letters before the post office closed, but the flood had already stopped access to the records.",
                    "Mrs Cole located a newspaper notice confirming that Samuel had moved to care for a relative. The team contacted the historical society in his new town, which forwarded a message through its public enquiry process. Samuel's daughter replied that he was alive and would be happy to speak about the reading room. She made clear that he could share memories without discussing the contents of any private letter."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "What the Letters Could Not Say", listOf(
                    "Samuel joined a video call with the library team. He explained that the reading room had been a place where neighbours helped one another practise reading, complete forms, and find books. The letters were likely invitations, thank-you notes, and requests for supplies. He had never learned what happened to them, but he remembered the volunteers' careful address book.",
                    "Dara asked whether he felt the letters should be opened. Samuel said that was for the recipients and archivist to decide. What mattered to him was that the volunteers' work had not vanished. He shared the names of several public events that could be confirmed in the minutes. The team added those events to the exhibit and credited the volunteers as a group, rather than guessing at anyone's private story."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "An Exhibit with Boundaries", listOf(
                    "The library opened the exhibit with the wooden box displayed beneath a clear cover. Visitors could read the register's public entries, view the photograph, and listen to Samuel's recorded memories with his permission. A sign explained that the letters remained sealed to protect the people named in them. The family attended and thanked the archivist for treating the collection carefully.",
                    "The community centre brought a list of its current learning programmes. It offered to host a new monthly reading afternoon in honour of the old room. Dara and Femi helped design a simple poster, making sure the event welcomed beginners and experienced readers alike. The letters had not been turned into a spectacle; they had helped people rediscover a useful tradition."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Address That Stayed", listOf(
                    "At the final planning meeting, the family donated the photograph and public minutes to the archive. The sealed letters remained under the archivist's care, with a written process for any future request from a verified recipient or lawful representative. The library catalogue recorded the box's journey from post office to archive and explained why the correspondence was not publicly available.",
                    "The first new reading afternoon filled the community centre with conversation. Samuel sent a message thanking the volunteers, and Dara placed a copy of the old photograph beside the new programme. The mystery had a clear answer: the letters were held because a community group lost its address during a difficult transition. Their contents were not needed to restore the group's story. The archive had protected both history and privacy, and the reading room's purpose had found a new address."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
